  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "A rational number can have a decimal representation that either *ends* or *repeats*.\n\nIn this lesson, we will see how to convert rational numbers written as fractions into decimals using *long division*. \nWe begin with examples where the decimal terminates. \n\nLet's convert $\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into a decimal using long division.\n\nTo write the fraction as a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n$$\n7 \\div 2\n$$\n\nWe proceed using long division as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td width=\"150\" align=\"center\">\n\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\"></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\,\\,3    {\\, . \\,} 5$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7   {\\, . \\,} 0}$</td> \n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$0$</td> \n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"15\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$0$</td> \n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{2} = 3.5.\n$$",
